          "question": "e-8203",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "Given a normal random variable $X \\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(X <       x) = P \\left( Z <       \\dfrac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $Z \\sim N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu = 2$ and $\\sigma = 2.$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.24) & =P(Z<\\frac{1.24-2}{2}) \\\\  & =P(Z<-0.38) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.38) \\\\  & =\u03a6(-0.38)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070250/assets/48d92806543cb86b2906032b1723fa942db842e7f283681cafeeb9b11fca8cab.png)\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(-0.38) = 0.3520.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(X <               1.24) = 0.3520.\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070250/example-8203.json",
          "file_sha256": "2b752314db7ee01bbd05ef35e3b177897172d0f59606a92ad948632511dbab7d"
