        {
          "question": "e-8205",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "Given a normal random variable $X \\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(a <        X <        b) = P \\left( \\dfrac{a-\\mu}{\\sigma} <        Z <        \\dfrac{b-\\mu}{\\sigma} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $Z \\sim N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu = 1$ and $\\sigma = \\sqrt 9 = 3.$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.13<X<3.25) & =P(\\frac{-1.13-1}{3}<Z<\\frac{3.25-1}{3}) \\\\  & =P(-0.71<Z<0.75)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070250/assets/a50513f550d8a3122e2f2276883a0189fe9bdc6dd19159e8ae94dbd5b247f5a9.png)\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $\\Phi(z)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.71<Z<0.75) & =P(Z<0.75)-P(Z\\le -0.71) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.75)-P(Z\\le -0.71) \\\\  & =\u03a6(0.75)-\u03a6(-0.71)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u03a6(-0.71) & =0.2389 \\\\ \u03a6(0.75) & =0.7734\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.71<Z<0.75) & =\u03a6(0.75)-\u03a6(-0.71) \\\\  & =0.7734-0.2389 \\\\  & =0.5345.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(-1.13<          X <          3.25) = 0.5345.\n$$",
